  "response": "팀원들이 전결을 기다리고 있다는 건 실제로 팀 전체가 멈춰 있다는 뜻이니까, 그 시간이 손해라는 말씀에 동의합니다.\n\n그럼 이렇게 가죠. 이 자리가 절차상 필요한 거라면, 최소한으로 채우는 것도 방법입니다. 돌아가셔서 팀 상황 처리하시는 게 지금 본인한테 가장 중요한 거라면, 그걸 존중해서 이 시간은 짧게 마무리하는 게 맞다고 봅니다.\n\n다만 한 가지만 확인하고 갈게요. 돌아가신 후에도 이 자리에 온 상황 자체—예를 들어 팀원들이나 상사가 이걸 어떻게 받아들일지—에 대해 신경 쓰이는 부분이 있으면, 다음에 다시 오셔도 됩니다. 그때는 지금처럼 절차를 채우는 자리가 아니라, 돌아가신 뒤 실제로 생긴 상황에 대해 이야기하는 자리로 쓰셔도 되니까요.",
  "reasoning": "The client is clearly resistant and feels this session is pointless. They're expressing frustration about being here when they have urgent work (team deadline, pending approvals). They've made their position clear: they want to finish quickly, they don't see value in the session, and they're experiencing real practical pressure.\n\nWhat would be helpful here? I should:\n1. Acknowledge their frustration without being preachy\n2. Respect their autonomy and boundaries - they've said they want to end quickly\n3. Not push further exploration when they've clearly stated they don't want to\n4. Perhaps offer a pragmatic compromise that respects both the requirement (they're here conditionally) and their stated need (time pressure)\n\nI should not:\n- Minimize their frustration\n- Push them to \"open up\" or find value in the session\n- Be overly optimistic or encouraging in a way that feels dismissive\n- Ask another exploratory question when they've clearly stated they want to wrap up\n\nThe right move is to respect their stated preference while also being honest about what's possible. I can acknowledge the real cost they're describing, validate it, and offer a practical path forward that honors both their need to go back to work and the fact that they're here. Maybe I can offer to make this brief and practical - perhaps helping them think about how to handle the return to work situation, or simply confirming that we can wrap up efficiently.\n",
